  "description": "Core A2–B1 gear for everyday conversation in broadly Latin American Spanish: be, exist, negate, ask, want, can, must, go.",
  "terms": [
    {
      "term": "ser",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "Says who or what someone is: name, job, home country. It also gives the time.",
      "example": "Soy Laura y soy profesora. — I'm Laura and I'm a teacher.",
      "mental_model": "Think of an ID card. Name, job, country: that is ser.",
      "anti_example": "Not for how you feel today. 'Soy cansada' is wrong; say 'estoy cansada'."
    },
    {
      "term": "¿qué?",
      "category": "Question word",
      "definition": "Asks 'what?' about a thing or an action.",
      "example": "¿Qué quieres comer? — What do you want to eat?",
      "discussion": "You don't need to change the word order for a question. Your voice goes up at the end. The accent on 'qué' marks the question."
    },
    {
      "term": "hay",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "Means 'there is' or 'there are'. The form is the same for one thing or many.",
      "example": "Hay un café cerca del hotel. — There's a café near the hotel.",
      "anti_example": "For a known, specific thing, use estar: 'El café está cerca', not 'Hay el café cerca'."
    },
    {
      "term": "no + verbo",
      "category": "Pattern",
      "definition": "Put 'no' right before the verb. That makes the sentence negative.",
      "example": "No tengo tiempo hoy. — I don't have time today.",
      "mental_model": "English adds 'don't'. Spanish just adds 'no'. Nothing else changes.",
      "discussion": "'No' also answers a question by itself. Then you often hear it twice: '¿Quieres café? No, no quiero.'"
    },
    {
      "term": "estar",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "The other 'be'. Use it for where something is and for how someone feels right now.",
      "example": "Mi hermano está en casa. Está enfermo. — My brother is at home. He's sick.",
      "mental_model": "Think of a photo of this moment. Place and mood: that is estar."
    },
    {
      "term": "querer",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "Want. Put a thing or a verb after it.",
      "example": "Quiero un taxi, por favor. — I want a taxi, please.",
      "discussion": "At a counter, 'quiero…' is normal and not rude in Latin America. For extra politeness, say 'quisiera…'."
    },
    {
      "term": "¿dónde?",
      "category": "Question word",
      "definition": "Asks for the place of a person or a thing.",
      "example": "¿Dónde está el baño? — Where's the bathroom?"
    },
    {
      "term": "tener",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "Have. Spanish also uses it for age, hunger, cold, and fear.",
      "example": "Tengo treinta años y tengo hambre. — I'm thirty and I'm hungry.",
      "anti_example": "For age, don't use ser: 'Tengo 30 años', not 'Soy 30 años'."
    },
    {
      "term": "tener que + infinitivo",
      "category": "Pattern",
      "definition": "Must, or have to. Put the basic form of the verb after 'que'.",
      "example": "Tengo que trabajar el sábado. — I have to work on Saturday.",
      "anti_example": "Don't drop 'que'. 'Tengo trabajar' is wrong."
    },
    {
      "term": "poder",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "Can: be able to, or be allowed to. The next verb stays in its basic form.",
      "example": "¿Puedo pagar con tarjeta? — Can I pay by card?",
      "discussion": "'¿Puede…?' to a stranger is a simple polite request: '¿Puede repetir, por favor?'"
    },
    {
      "term": "ir",
      "category": "Verb",
      "definition": "Go. The present forms look very different: voy, vas, va, vamos, van.",
      "example": "Voy al mercado los domingos. — I go to the market on Sundays.",
      "discussion": "Use 'a' for the place you go to. 'A' + 'el' becomes 'al'."
    },
    {
      "term": "ir a + infinitivo",
      "category": "Pattern",
      "definition": "Talks about plans and the near future. It works like English 'going to'.",
      "example": "Mañana voy a llamar a mi mamá. — Tomorrow I'm going to call my mom.",
      "discussion": "In conversation, this is the normal way to talk about the future. You can skip the other future forms for now."
    }
  ],
